{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["And the reason of <b>for HaShem your Elohim is testing you</b> — to say that the sign that came, which HaShem showed to this one in a dream or in his prophecy, was by the will of Elohim to test you in His love. I have already explained that the test is so called from the perspective of the one being tested. And He said <b>to know</b> — that what is in potential and disclosed before Him should become known in actuality. Now, the commandment of the entire pericah concerns a prophet who [comes] to serve idolatry; but concerning a prophet who prophesies in the name of HaShem to alter words of the Torah, our Sages interpreted it (Sanhedrin 90a) that if he comes to uproot one matter from the Torah, such as permitting the pig or one of the forbidden sexual unions, the same judgment is carried out upon him. But if he permits this for a limited time as a temporary ruling, such as Elijah on Mount Carmel, we are obligated to listen to him and to do everything he commands us. And it appears that if he prophesies in the name of HaShem to command a novel commandment, such as the reading of the Scroll [of Esther] (Megillah 14a), which does not incur the death penalty — rather, we are not to listen to him, as it is written (Leviticus 27:34), 'These are the commandments' — a prophet is not permitted to introduce anything new in them from now on. And perhaps, since we do not believe him, he is thereby a false prophet, and his death is by strangulation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:49:29.182216784Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}